 0.00658537307754159, 0.02554326504468918, 0.03923406824469566, 0.0013999209040775895, -0.0015873833326622844, 0.019883109256625175, 0.03681520000100136, -0.02376539632678032, 0.025035303086042404, 0.04997384920716286, 0.022253602743148804, 0.0014430070295929909, 0.017415862530469894, 0.008580940775573254, 0.040999844670295715, 0.0009153910796158016, -0.03746829181909561, 0.02007661946117878, -0.010582555085420609, 0.018419694155454636, -0.010751876048743725, 0.0070086754858493805, 0.015057465061545372, 0.006458382587879896, -0.021104639396071434, 0.02525300160050392]], "input": ["# Surgical Nutrition Research Community\n\nThe Surgical Nutrition Research Community comprises key entities focused on advancing knowledge in surgical practices and nutritional support for patients, particularly in the context of severe acute pancreatitis. The British Journal of Surgery serves as a central hub for research dissemination, while other journals and researchers contribute to a collaborative network aimed at improving surgical outcomes through nutrition.\n\n## British Journal of Surgery as a central entity\n\nThe British Journal of Surgery is a leading peer-reviewed medical journal that plays a pivotal role in the community by publishing significant research on surgical practices and clinical outcomes. It encompasses a wide range of topics, including studies on nutrition in acute pancreatitis, which is crucial for improving patient care. The journal's focus on gastric cancer and surgical nutrition highlights its importance in disseminating knowledge that can directly impact surgical practices and patient outcomes. Its relationships with other entities, such as the North American Surgical Nutrition Summit and various researchers, further solidify its central role in the community [Data: Entities (12310); Relationships (16616, 19088, 16109)]\n\n## Collaboration among journals\n\nThe British Journal of Surgery and Surg Gynecol Obstet collaborate by publishing studies related to surgical practices and nutrition in acute pancreatitis. This collaboration enhances the dissemination of knowledge across different medical specialties, allowing for a more comprehensive understanding of surgical nutrition. The interconnectedness of these journals indicates a shared commitment to advancing research in this critical area, which can lead to improved clinical practices and patient outcomes. The relationship between these journals is essential for fostering a collaborative research environment [Data: Entities (12309, 12310); Relationships (19087)]\n\n## Focus on nutritional support in surgery\n\nThe North American Surgical Nutrition Summit serves as a key platform for discussing best practices and consensus recommendations for nutritional support in surgical patients. This summit brings together healthcare professionals to share insights and establish guidelines aimed at improving the nutritional care of individuals undergoing surgery. The summit's relationship with the British Journal of Surgery underscores the importance of integrating nutritional support into surgical practices, which is vital for enhancing patient recovery and outcomes [Data: Entities (12645); Relationships (19088)]\n\n## Kalfarentzos F's contributions to enteral nutrition\n\nKalfarentzos F is a notable researcher whose work on enteral nutrition, particularly in severe acute pancreatitis, has been published in the British Journal of Surgery. His randomized prospective trial has contributed significantly to the understanding of enteral nutrition's role in managing this condition. Kalfarentzos F's research highlights the importance of evidence-based practices in surgical nutrition, which can lead to better patient management strategies and outcomes. His contributions are essential for advancing knowledge in this critical area of surgical care [Data: Entities (6896); Relationships (16109)]\n\n## Sitzmann JV's research on parenteral nutrition\n\nSitzmann JV is recognized for his research on total parenteral nutrition in patients with pancreatitis, with his findings published in Surg Gynecol Obstet. His studies focus on the use of total parenteral nutrition and alternative energy substrates, providing valuable insights into the nutritional management of patients suffering from severe acute pancreatitis. Sitzmann JV's work is crucial for developing effective nutritional strategies that can improve patient outcomes in surgical settings [Data: Entities (12305); Relationships (19086)]", "# National Kidney Foundation and Dialysis Outcomes Community\n\nThis community is centered around the National Kidney Foundation and the Dialysis Outcomes and Practice Patterns Study, both of which focus on kidney health and management. The entities are interconnected through various relationships that emphasize their collaborative efforts in improving kidney disease outcomes and providing guidelines for healthcare professionals and patients.\n\n## National Kidney Foundation's pivotal role\n\nThe National Kidney Foundation (NKF) serves as a central entity in this community, dedicated to promoting kidney health and managing kidney diseases. It provides comprehensive guidelines and resources that are essential for both healthcare professionals and patients. The foundation's efforts in enhancing understanding and treatment of kidney-related conditions are crucial, especially given the rising prevalence of chronic kidney disease. The NKF's guidelines are referenced by various studies and organizations, indicating its authoritative position in the field of kidney health [Data: Entities (4214); Relationships (9707, 13362, 13361, 13364, +more)].\n\n## Collaboration with Dialysis Outcomes and Practice Patterns Study\n\nThe Dialysis Outcomes and Practice Patterns Study (DOPPS) is a significant research initiative that investigates hemodialysis practices and outcomes. The DOPPS collaborates closely with the NKF, utilizing its guidelines to inform research and improve practice patterns in treating kidney failure. This collaboration highlights the importance of evidence-based practices in enhancing patient outcomes and underscores the interconnectedness of research and clinical guidelines in kidney disease management [Data: Entities (10106); Relationships (13363, 18157, 18164, +more)].\n\n## Research contributions from key individuals\n\nResearchers like Hecking E and McCullough KP play vital roles in advancing knowledge related to kidney disease management. Their contributions to the DOPPS focus on nutritional indicators and practices in dialysis patients, which are critical for improving patient care. The involvement of these researchers emphasizes the importance of academic and clinical collaboration in addressing the complexities of kidney health [Data: Entities (10114, 10115); Relationships (18165, 18166, +more)].\n\n## Interconnected guidelines for kidney health\n\nThe relationships between the NKF and other organizations, such as the American Diabetes Association and the Kidney Diseases Outcomes Quality Initiative, illustrate a network of guidelines that collectively aim to improve kidney health management. These interconnected guidelines ensure that healthcare providers have access to comprehensive resources that address various aspects of kidney disease, including diabetes-related complications [Data: Relationships (9707, 18157, 13362, +more)].\n\n## Focus on nutritional practices in kidney disease\n\nThe emphasis on nutritional practices within the DOPPS and its collaboration with the International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism highlights the critical role of nutrition in managing kidney disease. Understanding the nutritional needs of patients undergoing dialysis is essential for improving their overall health outcomes, making this focus a significant aspect of the community's efforts [Data: Relationships (18164, +more)].", "# International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism Community\n\nThe community is centered around the International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism, which collaborates with various organizations and researchers to improve nutritional care for patients with kidney disease. Key relationships include collaborations with Cano NJ and Ikizler TA, as well as partnerships with other organizations focused on kidney disease management.\n\n## Central role of the International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism\n\nThe International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism is the primary entity in this community, dedicated to enhancing nutritional care for kidney disease patients. This organization plays a pivotal role in developing guidelines and consensus statements that influence clinical practices. Its collaborations with other organizations, such as the Kidney Diseases Outcomes Quality Initiative and the Dialysis Outcomes and Practice Patterns Study, further solidify its importance in the field of renal nutrition. These partnerships enable the society to leverage collective expertise to improve patient outcomes through better nutritional management [Data: Entities (10107); Relationships (18158, 18163)].\n\n## Collaboration with Cano NJ\n\nCano NJ is a notable researcher within this community, recognized for his contributions to nutritional management in kidney disease. His involvement with the International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism highlights the integration of research and clinical practice. Cano's work on guidelines for enteral nutrition is particularly significant, as it addresses a critical aspect of care for patients with kidney disease. The relationship between Cano NJ and the society underscores the importance of evidence-based practices in improving patient care [Data: Entities (10112); Relationships (18169)].\n\n## Involvement of Ikizler TA\n\nIkizler TA is another key member of the International Society of Renal Nutrition and Metabolism, contributing to the development of guidelines and research in the field. His partic